# install/tool-versions.env — SINGLE SOURCE for the pinned OUT-OF-BAND tools.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Some of the shell stack is not in Debian/Kali apt, so bootstrap.sh installs it
# from upstream. That used to be five `curl … | sh` pipes: unpinned, unverified,
# and executed as the invoking user. On a box whose entire purpose is offensive
# work, "we ran whatever the vendor served today" is not a supply chain — it is
# the absence of one. Core solved the same problem for its CI toolchain in
# core/scripts/tool-versions.env; this is that discipline applied to bootstrap.
#
# HOW IT IS USED
#   bootstrap.sh's `verified_install` reads the *_VERSION and *_SHA256 below,
#   downloads the exact release ASSET (not the vendor's install script),
#   sha256sum -c's it, and only then unpacks the binary into ~/.local/bin.
#   A pin that is missing, malformed, or mismatched FAILS the install for that
#   tool — it never silently falls through to the unverified path.
#
# SCOPE — Linux x86_64 (amd64) only, the same scope Core's pin file declares.
#   These hashes are per-architecture. On any other arch bootstrap.sh skips the
#   verified path and says so; install those tools by hand.
#
# BUMPING
#   Edit the *_VERSION here, then run `scripts/update-tool-checksums.sh` to
#   refresh the matching *_SHA256, and review the diff. A version bump without a
#   checksum refresh is an incomplete change: the download will fail closed.
#
# NOT LISTED HERE, ON PURPOSE
#   starship — packaged by Kali (install/packages.txt). Prefer the distro.
#   yazi / viddy / ast-grep — built from source by `cargo install --locked`,
#     which does its own registry checksum verification via Cargo.lock.
#   doggo / carapace / sesh — `go install` against the module proxy, which
#     verifies against the checksum database (GONOSUMDB/GOSUMDB).
#   op (1Password CLI) — installed from 1Password's signed apt repo, which is a
#     stronger control than a pinned tarball (rotating signing key, not a hash).
#
# Plain KEY=VALUE, no spaces around `=`, no `v` prefix — so this both `source`s in
# bash and is greppable by a single sed. Same format contract as Core's file.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# atuin — shell history. Its own installer hardcodes ~/.atuin/bin, appends an
# `atuin init` line to ~/.zshrc that fights Core's 00-tools.zsh, and prompts on a
# tty for history import. Installing the release binary avoids all three.
ATUIN_VERSION=18.19.0
ATUIN_SHA256=4d7559ada42407ee8ddc62349acf134dd297568d032c45a746a7aef8a6860648

# mise — runtime/tool version manager. Core's 00-tools.zsh activates it.
MISE_VERSION=2026.8.6
MISE_SHA256=cfe49784ec9683b38510846958cfecd9b59da84d4e8a38d18ffda19dc2941ead

# uv — Astral's Python package/project manager.
UV_VERSION=0.12.5
UV_SHA256=68a509da24b06b4223a1c0175fb5eb5bc79342b76cbeff0cfe51ac3f5b17b6b2

# ty — Astral's type checker. Installed via `uv tool install` when uv is present
# (uv verifies its own downloads); this pin is the no-uv fallback path.
TY_VERSION=0.0.72
TY_SHA256=11087394fa6aeac8d449bce42a30eb03fd76dd9ad8f38e69dd8004785d3f98b0
